  "content": {
    "id": "p2-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Find all six ways to arrange four distinct points in the plane so that only two distances occur between any pair of points. Configurations that differ only by rotation, reflection, translation, or scaling are regarded as the same.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "sharedSolutionDiagramLayout": "row",
    "answer": {
      "short": "A square; a $60^\\circ$ rhombus; an equilateral triangle with its centre; an isosceles triangle with an interior point; a kite; and four vertices of a regular pentagon.\n\nThe diagrams specify the required shapes: blue segments have the shorter length and red segments the longer length.",
      "worked": "The six configurations are shown below. Every pair of points is joined; blue segments share one length and red segments share the other.\n\nOne exact construction for each is:\n\n1. Square: $(0,0)$, $(1,0)$, $(1,1)$, $(0,1)$. The two distances are $1$ and $\\sqrt2$.\n\n2. Equilateral triangle and centre: $(-\\frac12,0)$, $(\\frac12,0)$, $(0,\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2})$, $(0,\\frac{\\sqrt3}{6})$. The distances are $\\frac1{\\sqrt3}$ and $1$.\n\n3. Rhombus: $(0,0)$, $(1,0)$, $(\\frac12,\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2})$, $(\\frac32,\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2})$. The distances are $1$ and $\\sqrt3$.\n\n4. Isosceles triangle and interior point: $(-\\frac12,0)$, $(\\frac12,0)$, $(0,1+\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2})$, $(0,\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2})$. The distances are $1$ and $\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt3}$.\n\n5. Kite: $(-\\frac12,0)$, $(\\frac12,0)$, $(0,\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2})$, $(0,\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}-1)$. The distances are $\\sqrt{2-\\sqrt3}$ and $1$.\n\n6. Four vertices of a regular pentagon: the distances are its side length and its diagonal length. With side length $1$, the diagonal length is $\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}$.\n\nCoordinate differences and Pythagoras verify these lengths. These are the six configurations; an exhaustive proof is not required here.",
      "solutionDiagrams": [
